  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"146\" data-end=\"250\">The <strong data-start=\"150\" data-end=\"172\">Arsenic blood test</strong> measures the level of <strong data-start=\"195\" data-end=\"206\">arsenic</strong>, a toxic heavy metal, in the bloodstream.</p>\n<p data-start=\"252\" data-end=\"278\">It is primarily used to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"279\" data-end=\"668\">\n<li data-start=\"279\" data-end=\"317\">\n<p data-start=\"281\" data-end=\"317\">Detect <strong data-start=\"288\" data-end=\"315\">acute arsenic poisoning</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"318\" data-end=\"412\">\n<p data-start=\"320\" data-end=\"412\">Monitor exposure in occupational or environmental settings (e.g., smelting, pesticide use)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"413\" data-end=\"552\">\n<p data-start=\"415\" data-end=\"552\">Evaluate symptoms such as <strong data-start=\"441\" data-end=\"514\">nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, confusion, or peripheral neuropathy</strong>, when arsenic exposure is suspected</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"553\" data-end=\"668\">\n<p data-start=\"555\" data-end=\"668\">Differentiate between <strong data-start=\"577\" data-end=\"596\">organic arsenic</strong> (usually non-toxic from seafood) and <strong data-start=\"634\" data-end=\"655\">inorganic arsenic</strong> (toxic form)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>High levels of arsenic in blood may indicate:</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Cause / Exposure Source</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Significance / Effects</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Acute arsenic poisoning</strong></td>\n<td>Symptoms include nausea, vomiting, abdominal cramps, diarrhea, and cardiovascular collapse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Occupational exposure</strong></td>\n<td>In industries involving pesticides, wood preservatives, semiconductors, or mining</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Contaminated water or food</strong></td>\n<td>Particularly from groundwater in some regions (e.g., West Bengal, Bangladesh, parts of India)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Intentional or accidental ingestion</strong></td>\n<td>May present with GI symptoms, metallic taste, hypotension, or neuropathy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>High arsenic levels require <strong data-start=\"1716\" data-end=\"1747\">prompt medical intervention</strong>, including <strong data-start=\"1759\" data-end=\"1779\">chelating agents</strong> like dimercaprol or DMSA in severe cases.</p>",
